# A name is derived, not invented

An app called `Calculator` is the failure this rule exists to stop. The owner's complaint, recorded
2026-10-01: the agent building a utility "that I already hate" names it after its category, because
the category word is the first thing in its head, and every later stage then treats that thirty-second
decision as a given. A generic name is a **commercial** defect before it is a policy one: the title is
the strongest text-ranking field on Play, the bare category word is the head term a 1 B+-install
incumbent already owns, so the app is unsearchable at the one query it was named for, carries nothing
a user can prefer, and has no brand that can compound. The name is decided **at recon, recorded, and
derived** — like the promise list and the money plan, it is a build input, not a decoration applied at
the end.

The binding specification is `docs/app-naming.md`; the evidence is
`docs/research/2026-10-01-play-title-policy-and-aso.md` and
`docs/research/2026-10-01-app-naming-methodology.md`.

## Rules

- **The name is decided at recon, not after the build.** It lives in
  `docs/ideation/plans/<slug>.naming.json`, is validated by `scripts/new-slot.mjs`, and is written
  into the slot root as `naming.json` — beside `economics.json` and `differentiators.json`, before
  the first line of UI. The record is authored with the proposal, exactly as the promise contract is.
  A name written afterwards justifies only what was built.
- **A name is never invented; it arrives as a field.** The record carries **at least 5 scored
  candidates spanning at least 3 archetypes**, plus **at least one rejected alternative with its
  reason**, and includes the bare category word as a negative control that loses to every serious
  candidate. A single name with a defence is not a derivation, and a field that eliminated nothing
  was not generated. Score each survivor 0–5 on the seven weighted dimensions in `docs/app-naming.md`;
  require **≥ 70/100 with no dimension below 2.5**, and return to the field rather than defend the
  candidate that is left.
- **The rejection list is the enforcement surface, not advice.** Never propose a name that is a pure
  generic word or bare category noun (*Calculator*, *Notes*, *QR Scanner*, *Flashlight*, *Converter*);
  a `My` / `Simple` / `Easy` / `Pro` / `Free` / `Best` prefix or suffix; the factory's own name
  (*PlayKiln* or any variant); a number-only or emoji name; a name that promises a capability the app
  does not have; a name that collides with a competitor's; ALL CAPS, repeated symbols or a stylised
  misspelling; keyword stuffing; a name that fails the radio test (a listener cannot type it after
  hearing it once); a name with no room for the job noun or that overflows 30 characters in a longer
  locale; a name that needs a pronunciation guide or has more than one plausible spelling; a homophone
  trap; a name with an unexpected or culturally sensitive meaning in a declared locale; a name that
  duplicates another slot's head term; a name that only works in English; or a name that copies an
  incumbent's title with one word swapped. The full list is `docs/app-naming.md` § "The rejection
  list", and all sixteen are checked.
- **The name satisfies Play's rules, measured, not paraphrased.** The title is **30 characters or
  less, and full-width characters count the same as half-width** (Play Console Help; the Metadata
  policy and the best-practices page both repeat the cap). A title may not carry emoji, emoticons or
  repeated special characters; ALL CAPS unless it is the brand; keyword spam; another app's or
  entity's name; ranking or price text (`#1`, `Best of Play`, `10% off`, `Free`). Google states
  itself that unnecessary keywords *"will not impact ranking and provides a bad user experience"*
  (*Add preview assets*). Enforcement escalates rejection → removal → suspension → **account
  termination, in which every app in the catalog is removed**; removals, suspensions and warnings all
  count as strikes, and Google is not required to warn first. The quotes and their sources are in
  `docs/app-naming.md` § "Play's actual rules".
- **The name clears the same localisation bar the layout does, for the same reason.** The title is a
  user-visible string with a per-language variant and a hard cap: Play's field note says *"You can add
  one localized name per language"*, and *"the Metadata policy and our other policies apply to all
  translations"*. `en` and `ru` are the minimum. Measure `<Carrier>: <Job noun>` in every declared
  locale, keep **≥ 5 characters of slack**, and never let a machine translation name the app. A
  carrier that only works because English is short is the name-shaped version of the localisation
  defect `.cursor/rules/visual-identity.mdc` already fails.
- **The slug, the `applicationId` and the store title derive from the one recorded decision, so they
  cannot drift apart.** The chain is `name → slug → applicationId (com.gexetr.<slug>) → store title`;
  the scaffold and the listing are read from the record, not from separate flags, exactly as the theme
  and the store pack are both rendered from `store/studio.json`. The **`applicationId` is permanent
  and cannot be reused** — changing it after publish makes Play treat the upload as a different app,
  with no way to move installs, ratings or reviews. The **title stays editable** after publish, but by
  then the carrier is the brand. Before the first upload everything is reversible; that is why this is
  the last moment a name is free.
- **The contract is not in the approval digest's exclusion list, and must not be added to it.**
  Changing what the app is called changes what the owner is approving; that should lapse the approval,
  not slip past it.
- **Never edit a name to make it true, and never reword the rejection list to fit a favourite.** A
  name that fails a hard check is eliminated regardless of its score; if the best candidate collides
  or means something unfortunate in `ru`, the fix is to generate another candidate, not to lower a
  bar.

## How it is verified

- `node scripts/verify.mjs` — the **`naming.fixtures`** check runs in the **`invariants`** group, in
  the same process as every other core check, before any slot is read. Its inline fixtures drive the
  checker over synthetic records whose verdict is known, and include the legs that must fail: a bare
  category noun, a field spanning fewer than three archetypes, a title that overflows 30 characters in
  `ru`, a record with no rejection, and a missing negative control.
- `node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug>` — runs **`slot.naming`**, the per-slot half: schema, the
  score floor and the seven dimensions, candidate count and archetype spread, the rejection patterns,
  the measured per-locale title lengths against the cap and the slack, the `reconRef` file, the
  collision record, and that `slug` and `applicationId` agree with the directory and
  `registry/apps.json`.
- **A deleted file is `UNVERIFIED`, and `UNVERIFIED` is never a pass.** The check treats the decision
  as declared once it is on disk **or** in git, so deleting `naming.json` from the working tree does
  not switch the check off; a slot with no record at all is reported `UNVERIFIED` naming the path.
- A gate that cannot fail is not a gate. The failing fixture legs exist to prove this one can.

## The honest limit

No program can judge whether a name is **beautiful, clever or memorable** — a score of 82 is
arithmetic, not taste. The gate proves only that the decision was **derived** (a scored field across
several archetypes, an alternative rejected in writing) and **legal** (the rejections are absent; the
title fits every declared locale inside Play's cap with slack; the slug, package and title are one
decision). It cannot prove the name causes installs — no controlled experiment exists — and a clean
Play search plus a registry screen is not trademark clearance. The judgement that separates a
distinctive name from an ugly one stays with the **owner at checkpoint 1**, exactly as it does for the
visual identity and the promise list. This gate's job is to make sure he is asked, with the reasoning
on the table instead of a name an agent liked.
